# ----- One-time codes (optional) -----
#
# Route ONLY one-time codes to a different provider. Everything else always
# goes via Twilio: MSG91's current send API requires a registered template, and
# a template is not free text (DLT caps each variable at ~30 chars), so an
# arbitrary notification body cannot be expressed as one. A sender ID does not
# change that — it controls who the message appears to come from, not what may
# be sent.
#
#   ""       -> Twilio sends codes too (default)
#   "msg91"  -> MSG91 sends codes, Twilio still sends everything else
SMS_OTP_PROVIDER=""

# Required only when SMS_OTP_PROVIDER="msg91".
#
#   MSG91_AUTH_KEY        Settings -> API Keys. NOT the `tokenAuth` from an OTP
#                         Widget snippet: that is a public browser token and
#                         MSG91 rejects it, invisibly (sends still report
#                         "success"). Verify at Admin -> System -> SMS Providers.
#   MSG91_OTP_TEMPLATE_ID OTP -> Add template, using ##OTP## as the placeholder.
#                         OTP templates are approved instantly.
#   MSG91_SENDER_ID       OPTIONAL. Without it MSG91 uses its shared sender id;
#                         your own is only needed past ~2,000 msgs/month in a
#                         country, or for branding.
MSG91_AUTH_KEY=""
MSG91_SENDER_ID=""
MSG91_OTP_TEMPLATE_ID=""

# India only: DLT template entity id, if your MSG91 account requires it.
MSG91_DLT_TE_ID=""
# --------------- Notifications ---------------

# Redis — REQUIRED. The backend refuses to boot without it (exit 78, EX_CONFIG)
# and prints install instructions naming the host/port it tried.
#
# It is not an optional cache: sessions, rate limits, distributed locks, the
# cron scheduler (BullMQ) and cross-process settings invalidation all live in
# it. Earlier releases fell back to an embedded per-process store when Redis was
# missing, which could not coordinate anything between processes — two workers
# each believed they held the same lock and settings changes never reached the
# other. That fallback has been removed.
#
#   sudo apt-get install -y redis-server && sudo systemctl enable --now redis-server
#   redis-cli -h 127.0.0.1 -p 6379 ping    # expects: PONG
REDIS_HOST="127.0.0.1"
REDIS_PORT="6379"
REDIS_PASSWORD=""

# Which Redis logical database to use. Leave at 0 unless this Redis instance is
# shared with something else — BullMQ, the settings bus and the distributed
# locks all live in the SAME database, so pointing two installs at one Redis
# with the same index makes them fight over each other's locks and queues.
REDIS_DB="0"

# --------------- The cron scheduler: NOTHING TO SET HERE ---------------
#
# The scheduler runs in its OWN PROCESS by default. `pnpm start` brings up three
# PM2 apps — `backend` (serves HTTP, registers no jobs), `frontend`, and `cron`
# (registers every job, serves no traffic, port 4001). All three are defined in
# production.config.js, both halves in that one file so they cannot disagree.
#
# There is NO environment variable to set, and CRON_MODE deliberately does not
# appear in this file. It exists inside production.config.js, which sets it on
# each app; setting it here would only be able to half-configure something that
# is already whole.
#
# Why: every scheduled job is a BullMQ worker, and a BullMQ worker runs in the
# process that created it — on the same event loop, and in the same V8 heap,
# that serves HTTP. A job that grows the heap therefore stalls the site through
# garbage collection (a measured 1.9-second pause with the site answering
# nothing). A separate process has its own heap and is recycled on its own.
#
# The one supported opt-out, if you want the old single-process arrangement:
#
#   CRON_MODE="inline"
#
# production.config.js reads that and drops the `cron` app entirely, so you get
# one process that both serves and schedules — never a web process that
# schedules nothing next to a scheduler nobody started. Leave it unset (or
# absent, as above) for the default.
#
# Do NOT start a second scheduler by hand. `pm2 start production.cron.config.js`
# is retired and starts nothing; running two would execute every scheduled job
# twice against the same rows, withdrawals included.

# --------------- RUST_OWNS: leave this EMPTY ---------------
#
# The switch that makes this backend STOP DOING WORK. It exists for one
# situation, and if you are not in that situation the correct value is the empty
# one below.
#
# A second backend, written in Rust, is being brought up beside this one over a
# coexistence window measured in months. During that window both stacks point at
# the same MySQL, the same Redis and the same chains. For a read that is merely
# wasteful; for a WRITE it is the failure the whole arrangement exists to
# prevent — two withdrawal queues broadcasting the same signed transaction, two
# deposit monitors crediting one on-chain deposit. RUST_OWNS is how you tell
# THIS process which work the other one has taken over. Every token you add
# makes this backend do LESS.
#
#   RUST_OWNS=""
#
# Empty, unset, whitespace or a stray comma all mean the same thing: this
# backend does everything, exactly as it always has. That is the default and it
# is what you want unless a Rust scheduler is genuinely running and genuinely
# claiming these jobs.
#
# THE GRAMMAR. A comma-separated list. Each token is one of:
#
#   a cron job name, spelled as the admin cron page spells it
#     RUST_OWNS="processWalletPnl,ledgerArchive"
#
#   a loop token, for machinery that is not a cron job. The closed list is:
#     eco.withdrawals, eco.monitors, backgroundDepositScanner,
#     btcDepositScanner, spot.deposit.pollers, spot.fill-watcher,
#     nft.lifecycle, binary.settlement, ws.relay.all, vault.signing,
#     trading.bot, binary.ai, ai.market_maker, copy.queue, exchange.session
#
#   lease.<key>, for one engine lease — ecosystem-matching, futures-matching,
#     forex-trading, ai-market-maker, dex-confirmations, dex-pool-index — or
#     lease.candidate for all of them at once
#
#   eco.deposits.<chain>, e.g. eco.deposits.eth
#
#   eco.deposits.* — THE ONLY WILDCARD. `lease.*`, `eco.*`, `spot.*` and
#     `binary.*` are NOT wildcards: each is a token that names nothing, and the
#     boot log says so at error level. `all` and a bare `*` are refused
#     outright, because a typo must not be able to stand the whole stack down.
#
# TOKENS ARE CASE-SENSITIVE. `processwalletpnl` is not `processWalletPnl`. The
# Rust scheduler's parser does not fold case either, and if only one side folded
# a mis-cased token would stand this backend down for a job the other side
# refuses to claim — and the job would run NOWHERE.
#
# READ THE BOOT LOG AFTER YOU SET IT. Every token that names nothing is printed
# at error level, by name, with the near-miss when there is one. A token this
# backend does not recognise is a job still running here — or, if the Rust side
# spelled it correctly, one now running on BOTH.
#
# AND WATCH THE ADMIN CRON PAGE. A handed-over job is listed there as `refused`,
# with the reason, until the Rust scheduler reports a run of it. If it never
# does, you get an URGENT alert naming the job: a token this side honours and
# that side does not claim is a job neither stack runs.
RUST_OWNS=""

